"A cell has parts, just as your body has organs. A thin skin called the membrane holds the cell together and decides what goes in and out. Inside are tiny power stations called mitochondria. Remember how your cells burn sugar with oxygen to get energy? The mitochondria are where that happens. And in the middle sits the nucleus, the control room.": "84e38fa30cf6",
"A cell is the smallest part of you that is alive. It takes in food and oxygen, it uses energy, and it can make a copy of itself. Most cells are far too small to see: you could line up several of them across the width of a single hair. That is why nobody knew about cells until the microscope was invented. In 1665, an English scientist named Robert Hooke looked at a thin slice of cork and saw rows of tiny empty boxes. They reminded him of the small rooms where monks lived, so he called them “cells.”": "3059a062ac98",
"A trillion is a million millions.": "610b698f0504",
"An ant is tiny, but a cell is much smaller.": "200e2582fec7",
"How many cells do you have? A grown-up has about 30 trillion. A trillion is a million millions. If you counted one cell every second, without stopping to eat or sleep, you would need nearly a million years to finish.": "a02b5dae5087",
"Inside the nucleus is the most amazing thing of all: DNA. DNA looks like a very long, twisted ladder. The rungs of the ladder come in four kinds, and their order is a code, like letters in a book. This book holds the instructions for building you and keeping you running. Almost every one of your cells carries a full copy of it. If you could pull the DNA from just one cell out straight, it would be about two meters long!": "250b72c662b2",
"Look closely at the back of your hand. Your skin looks like one smooth sheet. But if you could shrink until a grain of sand looked as big as a house, you would see something surprising. Your skin is not one sheet at all. It is built from tiny pieces fitted together, like the stones of a path. Each piece is called a cell.": "0417cff81bfe",
"Look closely at the back of your hand. Your skin looks like one smooth sheet. But if you could shrink until a grain of sand looked as big as a house, you would see something surprising. Your skin is not one sheet at all. It is built from tiny pieces fitted together, like the stones of a path. Each piece is called a cell. A cell is the smallest part of you that is alive. It takes in food and oxygen, it uses energy, and it can make a copy of itself. Most cells are far too small to see: you could line up several of them across the width of a single hair. That is why nobody knew about cells until the microscope was invented. In 1665, an English scientist named Robert Hooke looked at a thin slice of cork and saw rows of tiny empty boxes. They reminded him of the small rooms where monks lived, so he called them “cells.” How many cells do you have? A grown-up has about 30 trillion. A trillion is a million millions. If you counted one cell every second, without stopping to eat or sleep, you would need nearly a million years to finish. A cell has parts, just as your body has organs. A thin skin called the membrane holds the cell together and decides what goes in and out. Inside are tiny power stations called mitochondria. Remember how your cells burn sugar with oxygen to get energy? The mitochondria are where that happens. And in the middle sits the nucleus, the control room. Inside the nucleus is the most amazing thing of all: DNA. DNA looks like a very long, twisted ladder. The rungs of the ladder come in four kinds, and their order is a code, like letters in a book. This book holds the instructions for building you and keeping you running. Almost every one of your cells carries a full copy of it. If you could pull the DNA from just one cell out straight, it would be about two meters long! Your cells are not all alike. A red blood cell is a soft red disc that carries oxygen. A nerve cell has a long wire for sending messages. A muscle cell is a long fiber that can pull. Yet they all follow the same book of instructions: each kind simply reads different pages. And every one of them came from another cell that split in two. That is how you grow, and how a cut heals. So what are you made of? Trillions of tiny living things, all working together. Zoom in on the model above and meet them.": "bb677860e147",
